\]

### 2. Calculate the relative speed of two HI molecules

For two identical molecules, the reduced mass is

\[
\mu=\frac{M}{2N_A}
=\frac{0.1279}{2(6.022\times10^{23})}
=1.062\times10^{-25}\ \mathrm{kg}
\]

Thus,

\[
\bar v_{\mathrm{rel}}
=\sqrt{\frac{8k_BT}{\pi\mu}}
=\sqrt{\frac{8(1.380649\times10^{-23})(666.85)}
{\pi(1.062\times10^{-25})}}
\approx 4.70\times10^2\ \mathrm{m\,s^{-1}}
\]

### 3. Calculate the collision cross section

\[
d=3.5\ \text{\AA}=3.5\times10^{-10}\ \mathrm{m}
\]

\[
\sigma=\pi d^2
=\pi(3.5\times10^{-10})^2
=3.848\times10^{-19}\ \mathrm{m^2}
\]

### 4. Apply the collision-theory expression

For the disappearance rate law, \(-d[\mathrm{HI}]/dt=k[\mathrm{HI}]^2\),

\[
k=P N_A(1000)\sigma\bar v_{\mathrm{rel}}
\exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right)
\]

where \(1000\) converts \(\mathrm{m^3\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}\) to \(\mathrm{L\,mol^{-1}\,s^{-1}}\).

The energetic collision fraction is

\[
\exp\left(-\frac{E_a}{RT}\right)
=
\exp\left[-\frac{1.908\times10^5}
{(8.314)(666.85)}\right]
=1.14\times10^{-15}
\]

Therefore,

\[
k=(1)(6.022\times10^{23})(1000)
(3.848\times10^{-19})(4.70\times10^2)
(1.14\times10^{-15})
\]

\[
k\approx1.24\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}
\]

Thus, the collision-theory value is approximately

\[
\boxed{1.2\times10^{-4}\ \mathrm{M^{-1}s^{-1}}}
\]

